# mt-vectors.golden — MT (MEMORY CHANNEL WRITE/TAG), Set direction
#
# ============================ SOURCE ============================
# Manual title as printed on cover:
#   "FTDX101MP / FTDX101D  CAT Operation Reference Manual"
#   (cover reads "FTDX101MP" / "FTDX101D" on two lines, then
#    "CAT Operation Reference Manual"; publisher "YAESU MUSEN CO., LTD.")
# Revision code as printed: 2308-L  (bottom right of the final printed page,
#   PDF page 26, alongside the Copyright 2023 notice)
# File consulted: docs/fixtures-private/manuals/ftdx101_cat_2308-L.pdf
#
# Position chart used:
#   Section heading "MT   MEMORY CHANNEL WRITE/TAG"
#   PRINTED page 16 (folio "16" at foot of page)  =  PDF page 17
# Chart direction the LAYOUT was read from: the ANSWER position chart
#   (the cleanly printed grid at the foot of the MT section).
# Chart direction the P7 VALUE was read from: the parameter legend line
#   "P7  Set: 0: (Fixed) / Read: 0: VFO  1: Memory" — the Set-direction
#   vocabulary was used because these are Set-direction vectors.
#
# ---- DERIVATION METHOD (as instructed, recorded verbatim) -------
# Every byte below was hand-derived by rasterising the PDF page with
#   pdftoppm -r 400 -png  and reading the rendered position charts as
# images (crops enlarged 160-600% with ImageMagick). NO text extraction
# was used (the PDF is in any case copy-protected: copy:no). The SET
# frames' byte layout was derived from the ANSWER grid's layout, with the
# SET direction's P7 value substituted from the legend, exactly as
# instructed.
#   CROSS-CHECK: the MT *Set* position chart on the same page was ALSO
#   legible in the 400 dpi render (it did not in fact overprint its
#   legend at that resolution) and its layout AGREES with the Answer grid
#   position-for-position across all 41 positions:
#     Set  row 1-10 : M  T  P1 P1 P1 P2 P2 P2 P2 P2
#     Set  row 11-20: P2 P2 P2 P2 P3 P3 P3 P3 P3 P4
#     Set  row 21-30: P5 P6 P7 P8 P9 P9 P10 P11 P12 P12
#     Set  row 31-40: P12 x10
#     Set  row 41-50: ";" at 41, remainder blank
#   Answer rows 1-41 are identical token-for-token. The only documented
#   difference between the two directions is the P7 vocabulary.
#
# ---- FIELD MAP read off the chart (positions are 1-based) -------
#   pos  1      : "M"                 (literal, chart)
#   pos  2      : "T"                 (literal, chart)
#   pos  3- 5   : P1  (3 positions)   memory channel
#   pos  6-14   : P2  (9 positions)   VFO-A Frequency (Hz)
#   pos 15-19   : P3  (5 positions)   clarifier direction (1) + offset (4)
#   pos 20      : P4  (1 position)    RX CLAR
#   pos 21      : P5  (1 position)    TX CLAR
#   pos 22      : P6  (1 position)    MODE
#   pos 23      : P7  (1 position)    Set: (Fixed)
#   pos 24      : P8  (1 position)    CTCSS
#   pos 25-26   : P9  (2 positions)   (Fixed)
#   pos 27      : P10 (1 position)    repeater shift
#   pos 28      : P11 (1 position)    (Fixed)
#   pos 29-40   : P12 (12 positions)  TAG characters
#   pos 41      : ";"                 (terminator, chart)
#   => total frame length 41 bytes including the ";".
#
# ---- PARAMETER LEGEND as printed beside the MT charts ----------
#   P0/1  001-099 (Memory Channel), P1L -P9U (PMS), 5xx (5MHz BAND),
#         EMG (EMERGENCY CH)
#   P2    VFO-A Frequency (Hz)
#   P3    Clarifier Direction +: Plus Shift, --: Minus Shift
#         Clarifier Offset: 0000 - 9990 (Hz)
#   P4    0: RX CLAR "OFF"    1: RX CLAR "ON"
#   P5    0: TX CLAR "OFF"    1: TX CLAR "ON"
#   P6    MODE 1: LSB  2: USB  3: CW-U  4: FM  5: AM  6: RTTY-L  7: CW-L
#              8: DATA-L  9: RTTY-U  A: DATA-FM  B: FM-N  C: DATA-U
#              D: AM-N  E: PSK  F: DATA-FM-N
#   P7    Set: 0: (Fixed) / Read: 0: VFO   1: Memory
#   P8    0: CTCSS "OFF"   1: CTCSS ENC/DEC   2: CTCSS ENC
#   P9    00: (Fixed)
#   P10   0: Simplex   1: Plus Shift   2: Minus Shift
#   P11   0: (Fixed)
#   P12   TAG Characters (up to 12 characters) (ASCII)
#
# ---- TAG PAYLOADS ----------------------------------------------
# The tag strings below are SYNTHETIC strings invented for this fixture.
# They are NOT real amateur callsigns and are not intended to resemble
# any issued callsign.
#
# ---- BYTE-BY-BYTE PROVENANCE -----------------------------------
# MANUAL-DOCUMENTED (chart or legend says so):
#   pos 1-2  "MT"          : Set/Answer grid, positions 1 and 2.
#   field WIDTHS of P1..P12 and the ";" at 41 : Answer grid (layout),
#                            corroborated by the Set grid.
#   pos 3-5  channel value : legend "P0/1 001-099 (Memory Channel)" —
#                            007 / 012 / 023 are inside 001-099.
#   pos 6-14 frequency     : legend "P2 VFO-A Frequency (Hz)"; the values
#                            chosen are Hz counts of plausible amateur
#                            frequencies (see assumption A2 re: padding).
#   pos 15   "+"           : legend "P3 Clarifier Direction +: Plus Shift".
#   pos 16-19 "0000"       : legend "Clarifier Offset: 0000 - 9990 (Hz)";
#                            0000 is the documented lower bound.
#   pos 20   "0"           : legend P4 0: RX CLAR "OFF".
#   pos 21   "0"           : legend P5 0: TX CLAR "OFF".
#   pos 22   "2"/"1"/"3"   : legend P6 2: USB / 1: LSB / 3: CW-U.
#   pos 23   "0"           : legend P7 "Set: 0: (Fixed)" — SET direction.
#   pos 24   "0"           : legend P8 0: CTCSS "OFF".
#   pos 25-26 "00"         : legend P9 "00: (Fixed)".
#   pos 27   "0"           : legend P10 0: Simplex.
#   pos 28   "0"           : legend P11 "0: (Fixed)".
#   pos 29-40 tag bytes    : legend P12 "TAG Characters (up to 12
#                            characters) (ASCII)" — the field is 12
#                            positions wide per the grid (29..40).
#   pos 41   ";"           : Answer/Set grid terminator cell.
#
# INHERITED ASSUMPTIONS (the manual does NOT state these):
#   A1  INHERITED-ASSUMED PAD BYTE. The pad character used to fill P12
#       when the tag is shorter than 12 characters is 0x20 (ASCII space).
#       The manual nowhere states what pads a short tag, nor whether the
#       tag is left- or right-justified within the field. Space padding
#       and left justification are my assumption.
#       LIFTED BY: a real-radio capture of an MT/MR Answer for a channel
#       whose stored tag is shorter than 12 characters — the bytes in
#       positions 29-40 of that Answer reveal both the pad byte and the
#       justification.
#   A2  Zero-padding of P2 to the full 9 positions. The legend gives only
#       "VFO-A Frequency (Hz)"; that a sub-100 MHz frequency is written
#       with leading zeros to fill positions 6-14 is an inference from
#       the fixed 9-position field, not a printed rule.
#       LIFTED BY: a captured Answer for any HF channel — the leading
#       characters of positions 6-14 show the padding directly.
#   A3  Pairing of "+" with a zero offset. The legend documents "+" and
#       "--" as directions but does not say which sign accompanies an
#       offset of 0000. "+0000" is my choice.
#       LIFTED BY: a captured Answer for a channel with the clarifier at
#       zero offset.
#   A4  Choice of channel numbers (007, 012, 023), frequencies
#       (14.250000, 7.100000, 21.300000 MHz) and modes (USB, LSB, CW-U).
#       These are free choices within the legend's documented ranges.
#       LIFTED BY: not applicable — these are vector choices, not claims.
#   A5  Tag content and character set. P12 is documented as "(ASCII)" but
#       the manual does not enumerate which ASCII characters the radio
#       accepts (e.g. whether an embedded space or a digit is legal in a
#       tag). Use of A-Z, 0-9 and space is assumed acceptable.
#       LIFTED BY: a real-radio round trip — MT Set a tag containing the
#       questioned character, then MT/MR Read it back and compare.
#   A6  That an unfilled P12 (vector 3) is legally expressed as 12 pad
#       bytes rather than by some other "cleared" encoding. The manual
#       does not describe clearing a tag.
#       LIFTED BY: a capture of an Answer for a channel with no tag set.
#
# ---- HARDWARE STATUS -------------------------------------------
#   UNVERIFIED. No frame in this file has been sent to, or captured from,
#   a real FTDX101MP or FTDX101D. Every byte is paper-derived only.
#
# Derivation date: 06/08/2026
# NO code, no generator, no other file in this or any repository, and no
# other document or web resource was consulted in producing this file —
# only the single PDF named above.
#
# NOTE ON TRAILING SPACES: vectors 2 and 3 contain significant trailing
# space characters immediately before the ";". They are frame content.
# Do not let an editor or formatter strip them.
#
tag_full_width_12	MT007014250000+0000002000000SYNTHTAG0001;
tag_short_space_padded	MT012007100000+0000001000000TESTTAG     ;
tag_cleared_all_pad	MT023021300000+0000003000000            ;
